   126	### Validated plan, cursor and decoder
   127	
   128	```rust
   129	// Private fields; callers cannot construct invalid state/shape values.
   130	pub struct Shape { actors: u16, orders: u8, hire_limit: u16 }
   131	pub struct GrammarPlan { shape: Shape }
   132	pub struct State {
   133	    shape: Shape, // Binds the cursor to its validated originating shape.
   134	    units: u16, orders: u8, hires: u8, slot: Slot,
   135	    unit: UnitPhase, market: MarketPhase,
   136	    quantity_present: bool, quantity_high_zero: bool,
   137	}
   138	// Private support-equivalence enums, not public action token enums:
   139	enum UnitPhase { None, Ordinary, Transfer, Plant }
   140	enum MarketPhase { None, Hire, Other, Seed, Product, Animal, Sell }
   141	
   142	pub fn plan(actors: i64, order_limit: i64, hire_limit: i64)
   143	    -> Result<GrammarPlan, String>;
   144	// Shape, State, UnitPhase and MarketPhase derive Clone/Copy/Debug/Eq/PartialEq/Hash.
   145	// GrammarPlan derives Clone/Copy/Debug/Eq/PartialEq.
   146	impl GrammarPlan {
   147	    pub fn shape(&self) -> Shape;
   148	    pub fn start(&self) -> State;
   149	}
   150	impl Shape {
   151	    pub fn actors(self) -> usize;
   152	    pub fn order_limit(self) -> usize;
   153	    pub fn hire_limit(self) -> usize;
   154	}
   155	impl State {
   156	    pub fn slot(self) -> Slot;
   157	    pub fn allows(self, token: i64, plan: &GrammarPlan) -> bool;
   158	    pub fn advance(self, token: i64, plan: &GrammarPlan)
   159	        -> Result<Option<State>, String>;
   160	    pub fn write_mask(self, plan: &GrammarPlan, out: &mut [bool])
   161	        -> Result<(), String>;
   162	}
   163	pub fn write_can_act(plan: &GrammarPlan, out: &mut [bool]) -> Result<(), String>;
   164	pub fn decode(plan: &GrammarPlan, tokens: &[i64], length: i64)
   165	    -> Result<serde_json::Value, String>;
   166	pub fn encode(plan: &GrammarPlan, action: &serde_json::Value, out: &mut [i64])
   167	    -> Result<i64, String>; // Claude review R1, below
   168	pub fn grammar_tables() -> Result<GrammarTables, String>;
   169	```
   170	
   171	`plan` validates `1 <= actors <= 241`, `1 <= order_limit <= 10`, `1 <= hire_limit <= 241` **before narrowing**. `hire_limit < actors` is valid and disables HIRE; it is not a shape error (`R:engine_rs/src/myolie_sampler.rs:73–99`; `R:tests/kaggriculture/test_model.py:440–479`). `State` is observation-local, resets at `start`, and cannot represent post-STOP state except as `None`. It retains its originating `Shape`: `allows` returns false on a different plan shape, and `advance`/`write_mask` return `Err("grammar plan/state shape mismatch")` before changing state/output. Equal-shape plans are interchangeable. `advance` checks `allows` before changing state; an invalid transition returns an error. `allows` returns false for any negative/out-of-width token before token-derived indexing. Mask output lengths must equal the current slot width or 252, respectively; check before touching output. `write_can_act` overwrites all 252 entries with `frame < actors + order_limit + 1`, independently of the selected early STOP (`W:docs/kaggriculture-contract.md:164–168`).
   172	
   173	For Task 1.4, one `(env, seat)` supplies a borrowed contiguous **3024-element i64 slice**, representing `[252,12]`, and the original i64 length. No i16 intermediate. Admission order is:
   174	
   175	1. Check exact slice size; check signed length in `[actors+1, actors+order_limit+1]`, hence at most 252. Do not cast length or calculate a slice end first.
   176	2. Scan every supplied element against its named slot's `0 <= value < width`, including the padding; reject `-1`, `65537`, `i64::MIN/MAX`, etc. No narrowing or clamping. Iteration ordinals, not unchecked token values, select widths.
   177	3. Require every frame at or beyond `length` to be all zero. These frames are then **ignored by transitions and decoding**. They are not additional NONE/STOP frames. See open question 2 about the reference's weaker padding admission.
   178	4. Walk exactly `length * 12` tokens through the state machine. Reject a terminal before the end, a missing final STOP, or any support/canonical-field violation. Only then render JSON. Input buffers are never mutated or retained.
   179	
   180	Task 1.4 derives the plan from the current legal own actor count/configured order limit plus explicit `hire_limit`, not from the program's claimed actor ids. It checks full NumPy shapes/dtypes/layout before selecting each seat slice and adds env/seat to errors. Decode all required seats before committing any game step; batch rollback and publication fences remain 1.4, not claims established by this pure decoder (`W:docs/kaggriculture-contract.md:233–236`; `R:engine_rs/src/training.rs:309–376`).
   181	
   182	**Canonical encoder (Claude review R1).** The plan makes the Rust grammar the single source of truth for C6 (`plan.md:63`, Task 1.5 "`codec.py` rebuilt over the Rust grammar"), and Task 5.1 must store v4 tokens for recorded replay actions (`briefs/5.1-bc-data.md:158–165`). Without a native encoder, 1.5/5.1 would have to re-implement enums, arities and HIRE capacity in Python, which is exactly the duplication C6 removes. Add the inverse of `decode`:
   183	
   184	```rust
   185	/// Write the canonical zero-padded [252,12] program for `action` and return its length.
   186	/// Strict: no normalization, defaults, PASS insertion, clipping or key synthesis.
   187	pub fn encode(plan: &GrammarPlan, action: &serde_json::Value, out: &mut [i64])
   188	    -> Result<i64, String>;
   189	```
   190	
   191	Admission: `out.len() == TOKENS_PER_SEAT` (checked before writing); the object has exactly the keys `farmer`, `hands`, `market` (missing or extra keys reject); `hands.len() == actors - 1`; every unit command is a nonempty array of the exact §4 arity with a supported uppercase name and item, and a JSON integer quantity in 1–1023 (transfers only); `market.len() <= order_limit`; every market entry is `[]` (EMPTY) or an exact §4 command with a JSON integer quantity in 0–1023; `null`, non-arrays, floats, strings as numbers, lowercase names and unit kind NONE reject. After building the tokens, `encode` runs them through the same `State` walk as `decode` (so HIRE capacity `A+h<H` and every support rule come from one implementation) and only then writes `out`; on error `out` is unchanged. Errors name the field, index and rule, with stable prefixes matching the reference codec's rejection categories (`unknown market command`, `unsupported unit command arguments`, `market item syntax`, `extra hand commands`, `market queue limit`, `quantity range`, plus `hire capacity`) so Task 5.1 can reproduce its admission table (`briefs/5.1-bc-data.md:185–199`). Round-trip law: for every admitted program `t`, `encode(decode(t)) == t`; for every admitted action `a`, `decode(encode(a)) == a`. Task 1.4/1.5 expose it as a PyO3 function over a caller-owned `int64 [252,12]` row; no JSON string hot path.
   192	
   193	**Live/inactive boundary:** all native observations are live under v4 auto-reset (`W:docs/kaggriculture-contract.md:166`). Thus native `decode` rejects length 0. H:63–68 permits all-zero, length-0 **synthetic inactive model rows**; Task 2.3 handles those without calling native decode. Do not add an implicit PASS interpretation or let native step use an untrusted live flag (open question 3).
   194	
   195	### Tables and Python handoff
   196	
   197	```rust
   198	pub struct GrammarTables {
   199	    pub unit_kind: [bool; 20],
   200	    pub unit_item: [[bool; 16]; 20],
   201	    pub unit_quantity_present: [[bool; 2]; 20],
   202	    pub unit_quantity_high: [[bool; 32]; 2],
   203	    pub unit_quantity_low: [[[bool; 32]; 2]; 2],
   204	    pub market_kind: [bool; 8],
   205	    pub market_item: [[bool; 16]; 8],
   206	    pub market_quantity: [[bool; 32]; 8],
   207	}
   208	```
   209	
   210	These eight fields contain **964 booleans**. Derive their rows by admitted `State` prefixes of `plan(1,1,241)`, following `R:python/owl/kaggriculture/gpu_sampling_grammar.py:53–98`; do not maintain another rule implementation. Initialize invalid unit kind rows 0/19 with singleton-zero item/present supports; kind admission remains mandatory. For low digits use `[present][high_is_zero]` with **true = index 1**. For absent quantity both low rows are `{0}` (one is only a placeholder). After each admitted market kind/item, compare high support with low support for **every admitted high digit**, and return `Err("market quantity digits differ")` if unequal; a Rust `assert_eq!` test also pins this invariant. This keeps release behavior fail-fast rather than relying only on a debug assertion. Source: `R:python/owl/kaggriculture/gpu_sampling_grammar.py:24–41,68–98`; H:29–52.
   211	
   212	Task 1.4's proposed initialization binding is `kaggriculture_grammar_tables() -> dict[str, numpy.ndarray]`, with **exactly these eight keys**, C-contiguous `numpy.bool_` arrays of these trailing shapes (no env/seat dimension). Metadata binding `kaggriculture_grammar_constants() -> tuple[int, tuple[str, ...], tuple[int, ...]]` returns `(GRAMMAR_TABLES_VERSION, SLOT_NAMES, SLOT_WIDTHS)`; initialization verifies version/names/widths before upload. The binding copies the native fields into owned arrays once; it does not derive masks in Python. Task 1.5's typed `GrammarTables` maps same-name fields to `torch.bool` tensors on the model device, once per model/device. Task 2.3 uses the exact H:31–41 indexing. No byte reinterpretation of Rust `bool`, persistent native cursor, plan cache, or per-action Python mask reconstruction.
