## What the real trainer consumes; L6 choice

| Current behavior, source | Consequence / owner of change |
|---|---|
| `VectorizedEnv` allocates pinned obs/reward/done tensors and retains NumPy views (W:python/owl/rl.py:329,339–377); `reset`/`step` return reused objects (:379,644,772) | 1.5 retains caller ownership but allocates two complete generations. Pinning request on CPU with no allocator support is an explicit error; CPU tests request false. Orbit'"'s existing warning/fallback remains untouched. |
| Trainer enables nonblocking copies only for CUDA+pinned env (:561); reset observation transferred at W:python/owl/train/ppo.py:564 | 3.1 records completion for the initial generation after that copy. |
| Policy sees "'`self._obs`, CPU actions pass through `_step_env`, then env.step at :878; rewards/dones H2D at :882–889; rollout stores prior obs/actions and current rewards/dones at :896–907; new obs copied at :912–916 | 3.1 records event **after** all these host reads for the generation, not immediately after env.step or before reward copy. `_RolloutBuffer.write_step` copies tensor values (:486–503); GAE must never retain a NumPy view. |
| `_actions_to_cpu` is blocking by default (:2273–2281), `_step_env` uses it (:2296–2301). No Event or explicit host-reuse synchronization is present in ppo.py | This incidental synchronization is not a portable lifetime contract across streams/refactors. Choose **two generations plus explicit event synchronization**, not an unproved synchronous baseline. |
| CPU `_obs_to_device` clones observations (:2242–2250), whereas `rewards.to(cpu)`/`dones.to(cpu)` may alias host arrays (:882–889) | Copy rewards/dones into rollout before generation reuse; for truncation clone CPU dones before setting artificial done flags, so returned native transition bytes retain their meaning. |
| Truncation reads the pre-reset subset and computes values (:975–985), then zeros reward (:987), sets dones (:988), invokes truncate but ignores its returned observation (:991) | 3.1 returns/adopts the fresh next_obs from truncate and fences both generations; 3.2 preserves Kaggriculture economic rewards (Orbit zeroing unchanged). No fabricated terminal metrics. |
| Rollout storage hardcodes Orbit player/entity dimensions (:270–464); action/mask maps are Orbit-only (:2057–2098), though schema field iteration exists (:2106) | 3.1 adds typed Kaggriculture unions/narrowing and storage; no adapter shoehorns data into Orbit slots. Sanctioned schema-field iteration is not dynamic game dispatch. |
| Rollout env constructed directly at W:scripts/run_ppo.py:154–161; evaluation env at :1281–1289. Evaluation accumulates shaped returns at :1365 and derives result at :1380, while reading terminal metrics at :1372 | 3.1 routes both constructors through 1.5 factory. 3.2 uses raw `bank_0/bank_1`/winner, never summed shaped rewards or reset obs.banks. 3.3 supplies `_evaluation_seed(base_seed,env_steps)`. |

Event protocol: initial A → next operation B → wait A'"'s last recorded event → next operation A. Adapter owns one event per generation, created lazily for CUDA; "'`record_copy_completion(obs, device=...)` resolves by **object identity**, rejecting an unknown batch or a stale generation after reuse. Record on `torch.cuda.current_stream(device)` after obs, masks, rewards, dones and any requested transition H2D copies. All readers use that stream or join their streams into it before recording. `Event.synchronize()` on the CPU is required before native writes; a CUDA `stream.wait_event()` alone would not stop CPU mutation. Repeated recordings must cover all readers, not replace an outstanding unrelated stream fence. No destructor sync substitutes for reuse fencing.

On CPU there are no CUDA objects or calls. A/B rotates on successful observe/reset/step/truncate only; a failed call leaves active generation and both published generations unchanged. A fence wait may complete on failure but cannot alter their bytes/native state. `record_copy_completion` is required at trainer copy sites even if an action transfer currently causes synchronization. Separate diagnostics retain cloned tensors, not indefinitely leased generations. This contract prevents host reuse hazards; it does **not** repair or explain Task 0.2'"'s Inductor GEMM overflow (W:cookbook/references/compiled-gemm-template-overflows-above-2-21-rows.md:16).

## Native transaction and publication

Isaiah provides the style, not these stronger semantics: shape checks/NumPy slices at W:src/rl/vec_env.rs:214,229,292; global Rayon live-state mutation at :1561; result collection :1674 and error conversion in W:src/rl.rs:139. There is no "'`allow_threads`/`detach` in this native env. Rebuild with an explicit owned Rayon pool and PyO3 0.28 **`py.detach`**. Hold all `PyReadonlyArrayDyn`/`PyReadwriteArrayDyn` guards for the entire method; capture only validated Send slices/owned candidates in detached closures, not Python objects. NumPy borrowing cannot fence CUDA DMA: Python generation events remain essential. Caller must not mutate/read borrowed output storage from another thread during the operation.

1. Under GIL acquire every typed borrow; validate **all** shapes, C layout, writeability, lengths of byte regions, checked size products and pairwise disjointness, including input/output overlap. Validate action slices'"' raw dtype/shape and per-seat signed length/ranges/padding in env then seat order. Preflight "'`ObsStaging` and destination environment counts. No writes.
2. Read current legal actor counts/order limits from cached validated current preparation; compute which envs will terminate from current step and `episodeSteps` using wide arithmetic. Reserve only those auto-reset seeds in ascending env index on a **local** copy of `next_seed`. Horizon is the kernel'"'s only terminal trigger (W:engine_rs/src/lib.rs:1487). Assert the later actual done flags agree; never reserve E seeds on a nonterminal step. For reset reserve all; truncate reserves selected envs only.
3. For every seat make "'`plan(actors, order_limit, hire_limit)` and call actual `decode(&plan, tokens: &[i64], length: i64) -> Result<Value,String>` (G:src/kaggriculture/grammar.rs:357,764). Input is the full borrowed `[252,12]` slice; no narrowing. Length bounds before usize conversion; all slots/ranges including padding, zero tail, then state walk/STOP. Decode **all seats before any engine step**. No Python serialization on live step.
4. Clone games into unpublished candidates; stage transition before-banks/counters and terminal slots. `ObservationGame` is Clone and wraps `Game` with immutable config (O:ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/briefs/1.3.md:238–265). Apply transition-safety admission below. `pool.install` zips candidate envs with `ObsStaging.buffers_mut().par_envs_mut()`; always use an explicit `ThreadPoolBuilder.num_threads(native_threads)`, including one thread.
5. Each worker catches unwinding panics with `catch_unwind(AssertUnwindSafe(...))`, advances only its candidate through `step_with_market_metrics`, captures finished transition banks/counters/reward/terminal record, and checks actual terminal timing. On done, replace candidate with `ObservationGame::from_seed` using its reserved seed, then `prepare()` the **new** game. Otherwise prepare stepped candidate. `prepare` owns one validated snapshot; `write_env` fills unpublished `ObsEnvMut` (two `ObsRowMut`s) infallibly. No worker publishes outputs or touches live games/seed counter/records.
6. Join all workers into `Vec<Result<...>>` in indexed env order; report the first failing env/seat deterministically only **after all workers join**. Also catch coordinator/pool-install unwinds before publication. Any failure drops candidates and reusable scratch is considered dirty/unpublished. Reset/construction/observe preparation errors follow the same rule. Process abort/OOM/kill is outside recoverable `catch_unwind`; do not claim survival after process death.
7. With GIL, allocate the small return metrics dict **before** publication (only aggregate lists such as `total_games_played`, `terminal_bank_0`, `terminal_bank_1`, `terminal_margin_0`, in ascending terminal env order; empty lists on no terminal). Prebuild native terminal records and seed vectors. Native full terminal dicts stay lazy. Check all conversions, including econ u64→i64, before this point.
8. Publish `ObsStaging::publish(&mut ValidatedObsBuffersMut)` first. Its only error is the E-equality guard **before its first copy** (O brief:313–318); preflight already proved it. The first array copy is the commit point. All remaining observation copies, transition `copy_from_slice`s and `mem::swap` installation of games/prepared state/seeds/counter/terminal records are infallible and nonallocating. No Python dict construction, cloning, error conversion, checked arithmetic or `?` after the first write. Drop old allocations only after the commit. Cache the published transition for observe/truncate. Test a deliberately late observation error to prove no early publication.

`Game::step_with_market_metrics` already clones internally and assigns only on success (W:engine_rs/src/lib.rs:1443–1449); an ordinary failed step does not partially mutate that Game. Outer candidates are still required because other envs may succeed before another fails, and reset/encoding/panics can fail later. Baseline cost therefore includes **outer clone + kernel inner clone + prepare snapshot**, not “one clone”. Measure each separately in an optimized, bounded one-env probe (same snapshot timing discipline as 1.3): 20 warmups, at most 200 samples, stop at 120 s / 1 GB; early and dense constructed states are separate workloads. Source/build/profile/hashes, elapsed and allocation/RSS estimates go in `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/1.4/timing.json`. At 128 envs/rank Claude measures equivalent full-update phase costs; reopen an undo/staging optimization only if cloning is an observed dominant cost, preserving rollback tests. No invented speed threshold or kernel edit.

## Seeds, resets, terminal timing and truncation

Constructor consumes seeds before any explicit reset: E=2, seed=11,stride=2 creates [11,13], next=15; observe consumes zero; reset creates [15,17], next=19. Truncate [false,true] consumes 19 only. Seed overflow rejects the **entire** operation (including a final consumed seed whose subsequent checked increment cannot be represented). Different env worker scheduling cannot change assignment order. Rank streams base+r+k*world_size are disjoint for valid ranks before overflow; the old `(base+rank*n_envs)+k` overlaps, e.g. E=2 rank0'"'s third seed equals rank1's first. The reference source at its pin already has the fix (R:scripts/run_ppo.py:164–165); the rejected formula is recorded at R:cookbook/references/shared-ppo-adapts-game-batches-without-a-second-loop.md:46, not falsely attributed to that current runner.

Default kernel "'`episodeSteps=720` finishes on transition **719**: condition uses previous step >= episodeSteps−2 (W:engine_rs/src/lib.rs:701,1487). Read `terminal_banks()` / snapshot done to detect completion, assert horizon prediction; episodeSteps=1/2 both finish after one transition. Terminal records capture `bank_0`, `bank_1`, `margin_0`, i64 `episode_steps`, integer `winner` from raw bank ordering (0,1,−1 tie), `econ_0/econ_1` each `[32]` i64. `margin_0` must also be finite. They last until the next successful step or selected explicit reset; failed calls retain them. `step` outputs both dones true and completed reward/counters/banks while returned obs.banks/masks/clock are from exactly one newly seeded game. Never derive terminal results from reset obs or shaped reward (L1).

`truncate_envs` has `clear_transition=False` fixed. Preserve the prior transition'"'s reward/done/bank/econ bytes in the new output generation; native dones remain genuine termination, not a fabricated winner. Selected terminal records clear; unselected records persist. The trainer must (1) copy the pre-reset returned observation/subset to its device; (2) evaluate bootstrap from it, retaining its tensor lifetime; (3) finish/record queued reads of that generation; (4) call truncate; (5) replace "'`next_obs` with returned reset batch; (6) copy that batch and record its completion. It stores separate artificial truncation/done flags and bootstrap values for GAE. It does not ask native to replay the just-earned economic reward. See W:python/owl/train/ppo.py:975–994; Task 3.2 removes reward zeroing for Kaggriculture only. A one-step death/ineffective-command reward followed by truncation must survive, with bootstrap distinguishably based on the old clock/bank values and no terminal winner.

Evaluation factory uses an independent base computed by Task 3.3'"'s "'`_evaluation_seed(base_seed,env_steps)` and its own native counter. Validate i64 range including construction and future consumption; never reset/reuse rollout'"'s seed state to evaluate. This specifies independent lifecycle/reproducible evaluation identity, not a promise of infinite disjointness between every evaluation and training stream inside one finite integer domain. Record bases/strides/consumption for audit.

## Reward ownership and arithmetic

"'`src/kaggriculture/reward.rs` defines `RewardConfig { reward_mode: RewardMode, econ_shaping: f64, econ_starvation_weight: f64, econ_drought_weight: f64, econ_cap: f64, econ_ineffective_weight: f64, econ_ineffective_cap: f64 }`, with `RewardMode::WinLoss` only. Extract a plain exact-key `PyDict`; do not reflect on an arbitrary Python config object. This makes the hot path independent of Python and enables unambiguous missing/extra-key tests. Python serializes its typed validated config once.

For seat s/opponent o, `S=ECON_STARVATION=0`, `D=ECON_DROUGHT=1`, `I=ECON_INEFFECTIVE=2`; `W=reward_config.econ_shaping` is a **coefficient, not an economic counter**. The 32 counters and reader are W:engine_rs/src/lib.rs:1118–1175,1370. `econ_attrib.rs:1–9,54,84` defines a separate **167-counter telemetry ledger**, never part of reward. Historical v4 counters 10–31 do not silently acquire reward weights.

```text
P(c) = min(econ_cap, W*(econ_starvation_weight*S + econ_drought_weight*D))
     + min(econ_ineffective_cap, econ_ineffective_weight*I)
delta_s = P(after_s) - P(before_s)
terminal_scale = 1 - (econ_cap if W>0 else 0)
                   - (econ_ineffective_cap if econ_ineffective_weight>0 else 0)
r_s = delta_o - delta_s + done*terminal_scale*sign(bank_s-bank_o)
```

Coefficients required, finite, nonnegative; active caps <1 in sum; `reward_mode==win_loss`. Zero inactive caps permitted. Avoid `0*inf` intermediate NaNs: short-circuit disabled components, use capped/safe multiplication for large counts, reject remaining nonfinite outputs. gamma=1 is **trainer config validation in Task 3.2/3.4**, not an unmentioned constructor parameter. 1.5 ports config/test intent from R:python/owl/kaggriculture/rewards.py:19,74 and R:tests/kaggriculture/test_env.py:168–210; there is no standalone reference test_rewards.py. Remove unsupported historical reward modes/share_weight and default coefficients explicitly.

Native f64 penalty deltas follow the reference arithmetic, then cast the economic difference to f32; on terminal add the terminal term to that f32 promoted back to f64, then cast once to f32, matching R:engine_rs/src/training.rs:403–419. The Python oracle expresses the formula independently and reproduces these two output rounding points. Production has one implementation (Rust); Python oracle is never invoked by step or substituted on error.

Starting counters are zero, cumulative penalties lie between zero and active caps, so the full **undiscounted, untruncated episode** telescopes to opponent penalty minus own plus terminal_scale*sign, with absolute return <=1 in real arithmetic. Test exact telescoping f64 oracle and stored f32 sum with only a rounding budget `sum(0.5*ulp(output rounding terms))`, not an unexplained 1e-3 tolerance. Bootstrap-augmented partial returns/GAE targets need not satisfy this bound. Owner'"'s W=0.2 belongs to Task 3.4's explicit recipe, not a native default or evidence of optimality (W:ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/plan.md:259).

## Transition-safety admission (1.3 Q4)

There is no native load-state/from_header lifecycle API. Imported headers remain 1.3's observation-only "'`encode_kaggriculture_headers_into` surface for 5.1. Unit tests can build private test fixtures through `ObservationGame::from_header`; this does not authorize stepping arbitrary caller states in production.

Seeded state plus individually representable i64 fields is **not** a proof of safe arithmetic: shed/carried sums use i64 at W:engine_rs/src/lib.rs:3170,3235,3893,3918,4407–4410,4431; inventory additions at :2942 and day+yield offsets at :2965; economic and attribution ledgers also use unchecked additions (:1460; W:engine_rs/src/econ_attrib.rs:151,172). Observation 1.3 may encode wider intermediate totals; lifecycle must reject unsafe transitions before committing them.

`admission.rs` computes all current shed/carried/seed/tile aggregates in i128/u128, checks conversion and nonnegativity, `step+1`, and day headroom through max crop yield offset +1 (currently 13). Conservatively bound the next goods total using current shed+carried, harvestable tile stock, one fertilizer collection per actor and at most 1023*M market incoming units, with checked wide sums; bound per-item seed additions by 1023*M. Also check terminal held-goods + held-tile-yield and seed totals before narrowing. Name each checked kernel expression in tests. No arbitrary `shedCapacity`/`episodeSteps` cap outside the accepted envelope.

These aggregate guards do not cover every hidden telemetry addition or float-to-int cast. Require root **release** package profile `overflow-checks = true` for `kaggriculture-engine`, keep panic=unwind, and catch worker panics transactionally. This changes build policy, not vendored bytes. Add a release regression using an explicit test-only near-overflow state to prove the dependency is actually compiled with checked overflow; record `cargo tree`/profile and build identity. Check public econ/attrib counters before/after for monotonicity and checked u64→i64 publication; reject nonfinite money/metrics. Profile overhead later; do not disable checks for speed. Reopening question Q3 covers whether Claude prefers a fully enumerated action-specific arithmetic proof instead. **No acceptance claim that the simple goods bound alone proves the whole kernel safe.** A cast/saturating operation is not caught by `catch_unwind`; inspect and add explicit admission for reachable such expressions before qualifying huge-config transitions, and retain a precise unsupported-state error if that audit finds an unrepresentable case.

## Grammar bindings and downstream consumers

Actual G interfaces used: `plan(i64,i64,i64)->Result<GrammarPlan,String>` (:357); `State::slot`, `allows`, consuming `advance`, `write_mask` (:380,384,446,534); `write_can_act(&GrammarPlan,&mut[bool])->Result<(),String>` (:553); `decode(&GrammarPlan,&[i64],i64)->Result<Value,String>` (:764); `encode(&GrammarPlan,&Value,&mut[i64])->Result<i64,String>` (:902); `grammar_tables()->Result<GrammarTables,String>` (:598). Encode stages output and publishes only after canonical validation (:967). Codec borrows full 3024 i64 values, admits length before narrowing, then padding/support in the same order as native live step. Never accept zero live length as an inactive no-op.

